WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.169
So now that we know how to create UI elements, and we know how to animate them,

2
00:00:05.169 --> 00:00:11.169
let's talk about how we can go from that flat UI element on our screen to 3D in-world UI elements.

3
00:00:11.169 --> 00:00:15.169
The widget component is the first step in creating our UI elements in 3D.

4
00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:21.420
Simply put, the widget component manifests 3D UI elements created through UMG into the world.

5
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:27.969
The widget component itself is a 3D instance of the widget blueprint that you can interact with.

6
00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:31.969
The component offers a great deal of properties that we can leverage as developers.

7
00:00:31.969 --> 00:00:34.969
However, some of the key properties that we should take a look at are space.

8
00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:37.969
This is the coordinate space in which to render the widget.

9
00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:40.969
The choices here would be world or screen.

10
00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:43.130
We've then got the widget class.

11
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.130
This is the class of user widget that will be created and displayed.

12
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:51.479
From there we've got draw size, which is the size of the displayed quad.

13
00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:55.479
Next we've got window focusable, and this determines whether the virtual window created

14
00:00:55.479 --> 00:00:58.479
to host the widget is focusable by the user.

15
00:00:58.479 --> 00:01:01.479
More specifically, should this take the focus from the user.

16
00:01:01.479 --> 00:01:03.899
And finally we've got the pivot.

17
00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:09.900
This is the pivot point that the widget will use when placed relative to the position of the component.

18
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.209
Having the UI in the world is all well and good, but how do we interact with it?

19
00:01:15.209 --> 00:01:18.209
The widget interaction component has got you covered.

20
00:01:18.209 --> 00:01:23.500
The widget interaction component allows for players to interact with the UI that exists in the 3D game world,

21
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.500
but not on the viewport.

22
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.909
This component is added to the player pawn or the character,

23
00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:31.909
and serves as the physical location for the trace start.

24
00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:35.909
In the example above, we can see that the trace has been fired from the tip of the gun.

25
00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:39.909
In this regard, using the widget interaction component makes dealing with UMG

26
00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:43.909
in immersive virtual reality experiences much more viable.

27
00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:47.909
We can attach an interaction component to each handheld controller.

28
00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.709
The actual interaction itself is done by simulating a defined key press.

29
00:01:52.709 --> 00:01:56.709
The component itself can be set up to simulate different types of input methods

30
00:01:56.709 --> 00:02:00.709
such as press, release, or both press and release at the same time

31
00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:03.709
if we wanted to simulate a button click for instance.
